<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan de Lanuza y Urrea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1591</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Juan de Lanuza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan de Lanuza y Urrea was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-of-aragon">Justicia of Aragon</a>, the judge charged with upholding the kingdom's fueros against royal power. When Philip II tried to seize his fugitive secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-perez">Antonio Pérez</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1591, Lanuza called the Aragonese to resist; a royal army entered the city, and he was beheaded without trial in December 1591. He became a symbol of Aragonese liberties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-lanuza-y-urrea.jpg" alt="Juan de Lanuza y Urrea" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo López del Plano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Last_moments_of_Juan_de_Lanuza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
